      family: "economic",
      requires: ["an underlying primitive that is unprofitable at small size and profitable at borrowed size", "a lender with the required asset and liquidity at the fork block, and a knowable fee", "a path that stays open inside a single transaction (no per-block or epoch guard)", "a fork with the real pools, the lender, and the target deployed"],
      questions: ["What is the underlying primitive, and at what size does it become profitable relative to the lender's available liquidity?", "Does the primitive require holding a position across blocks, or can the whole sequence complete inside the loan callback?", "Which lender supplies the capital and what is its fee: does net profit survive that plus swap fees?", "Is the vulnerable path protected by a per-block, per-epoch, or cooldown guard a single-transaction attack cannot satisfy?", "Does the protocol pay out based on a value the same transaction just changed, such as a live balance, reserve, or share price?", "Is the profit taken from the protocol or another user, or merely from a third-party pool, which would be arbitrage and not a vulnerability?", "Can the attacker repay the loan and keep a positive balance in the same transaction, with the repayment shown in the PoC?", "If an asset must be swapped mid-attack, does that swap move the price the protocol trusts, compounding the effect?"],
      false_positives: ["A flashloan was used with no net profit number, proving only that borrowing works", "Profit originating from a public AMM rather than the protocol, which is arbitrage", "A primitive that requires the position to persist across blocks, beyond a flashloan's reach", "Loan and swap fees exceeding the edge, for example five basis points of a large notional against a rounding leak", "Liquidity that is not actually available at the fork block for that asset", "The path reverting under the loan callback due to a same-block guard the report did not read", "A self-funded zero-capital claim where the test seeds the attacker with the capital it claims to borrow", "A liquidation bonus observation where the profit is the intended bonus"],
      poc_must_show: ["the lender, asset, borrowed amount, and the fee actually charged", "capital in from the lender and the repayment, with the attacker balance delta after repayment strictly positive", "a control run with unborrowed small capital showing the delta is zero or negative, proving the loan was necessary", "the protocol or victim loss in the same unit, so the profit has a source", "the fee breakdown (loan fee, swap fees, gas) subtracted from gross", "no privileged role and no pre-seeded attacker balance"],
      impact_bar: "Reportable for a zero-capital single-transaction sequence extracting value from the protocol or its users with net profit positive after all fees, and a control run proving the loan was necessary. Informational when it only works with the attacker's own capital, nets negative after fees, or is third-party arbitrage with no protocol loss.",
